---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/* THE DESTINATIONS, WITH WHAT IS AT THEM.
   This page used to end with five nav words on one line and then a third of
   a screen of dead navy — 354px at 1440, 158px at 390, the largest band on
   the site, across which the ground moves 0.28 L*. `held-space.mjs` called
   it a hole and it was one. It is also the ONE route a reader reaches by
   accident: they mistyped, or followed a stale link, or were forwarded a URL
   that moved. A row of five words tells such a reader nothing about which of
   them to try.
   So the row is a ledger. Each line is that page's OWN meta description,
   already on the site and already traced — nothing here is authored for this
   page. Home's line is the brief's site map for the homepage (mission line,
   four pilots as cards, the Forum as one scene). */
const dests = [
  { href: '/', label: 'Home', line: 'The mission, the four pilots, and the Forum.' },
  { href: '/institute/', label: 'Institute', line: 'How the Institute works: short pilots, measured outcomes, published results.' },
  { href: '/pilots/', label: 'Pilots', line: 'Four active pilots for 2026: what each one is, who it is for, and where a partner comes in.' },
  { href: '/forum/', label: 'Forum', line: 'An annual invitation-only convening at the Kennedy Compound, Hyannis Port.' },
  { href: '/people/', label: 'People', line: 'Two founding partners, and sixteen names put forward as a prospective board.' },
];
---
<Base title="Page not found" description="That page does not exist.">
  <section class="nf">
    <div class="shell nf__in">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>404</p>
      <h1 class="display display--poster lines">That page isn’t here.</h1>
      <p class="bridge" data-reveal="220">It may have moved, or it may never have existed. The rest of the site is below.</p>
      <ul class="nf__list" data-seq="90">
        {dests.map((d) => (
          <li data-reveal>
            <a class="nf__row" href={d.href}>
              <span class="label label--lit nf__k">{d.label}</span>
              <span class="nf__v">{d.line}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .nf {
    /* NO min-height. It was 78svh, and it was a second reserve under the
       same gap, invisible at the viewport everything here is measured on.
       With `align-content: start` every pixel of the stretch lands BELOW the
       content, on top of the tail below and the footer's reserve below that.
       It binds whenever 78svh exceeds this section's natural height (968px
       at 1440 after the tail change), i.e. above about 1241px of viewport:
       at 1440x1400 the crossing measured 308.8px — 18.4% of a 1680px route,
       WORSE than the 259.6px this file was opened to fix, and unreachable
       from the 1440x900 and 390x844 boundaries every gate here samples.
       It also bought nothing it was there for: the document is 1556px at
       1440 with the footer in it, so it scrolls at every viewport height
       this rule could fire at, and the footer was never going to float.
       Removed; the crossing is 185.0px at 1440x900 AND at 1440x1400. */
    display: grid;
    /* The house shelf, like every other inner page: --head-pad under the bar,
       content starting on it. This page hand-rolled its own top pad and then
       vertically centred inside 78svh on top of it, which opened it 64px
       below /pilots/ and /people/ — the one route a reader arrives at when
       something has already gone wrong was also the one that did not look
       like the rest of the site. */
    align-content: start;
    /* THE TAIL, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE HOUSE TAIL.
       This was clamp(4rem, 9vw, 8rem) — 128px at 1440, 64px at 390, the
       LARGEST last-section tail on the site — and the footer reserves
       133.6px on top of it (Footer.astro, .foot padding-block start). Those
       two constants stack, and the sum is what a reader crosses: 259.6px
       between the list's closing rule at 914.8 and the WORK WITH US eyebrow
       at 1176.4, on a 1630px route. That is 15.9% of the shortest page on
       the site, against 1.5-7.0% on the SIX other routes. Measured
       mark-to-mark at 1440x900, scripts on, reduced motion — the gap from
       the bottom of the last mark outside the footer to the top of the
       footer's first mark, over the document height, all seven routes:
       /pilots/ 81.2 = 1.5%, / 208.8 = 3.3%, /partner/ 157.1 = 4.6%,
       /people/ 206.1 = 4.9%, /forum/ 188.9 = 6.4%, /institute/ 214.0 = 7.0%,
       /404 259.6 = 15.9% before this change and 185.0 = 11.9% after.
       THE FRAME THIS NOTE USED TO STATE THAT IN WAS WRONG, and the
       correction is worth more than the digits. It said "the five other
       routes that carry the same footer reserve" and then listed /404 as one
       of the five — four others, not five — and the two routes it dropped
       are exactly the two that do NOT carry the shared reserve. There are
       three footer reserves on this site: 133.6px at 1440 on /, /institute/,
       /forum/, /people/ and here; 83.2px on /pilots/, which scopes its own
       (pilots.astro, `main:has(.pd) + footer.foot`, argued there); 88.96px
       on /partner/, whose footer is the coda variant, has no eyebrow, and
       opens on the coda rule. Quoting a spread over the subset that happens
       to share one constant, without saying it was a subset, is how
       "3.3-7.0%" came to stand for a site whose spread starts at 1.5%.
       What survives the correction is the argument, which never turned on
       the two dropped routes: across the five that DO carry 133.6px the
       reserve is identical, so the spread among those five is the SECTION
       tails, 0 to 128px. Moving the shared constant shifts all five equally
       and leaves /404 the outlier it is. So the constant that is wrong here
       is this one, not the shared one. And this page has the most definite
       ending on the site — a hairline across the full measure, the list's
       own bottom border — after which less reserve is owed, not more. 53.4px
       at 1440 puts the crossing at 185.0px: the tightest of the five that
       share the reserve, which is right for the shortest route. /pilots/ at
       81.2 and /partner/ at 157.1 are tighter still, and neither is a
       comparison this constant can be set by — they reserve less to begin
       with.
       ONE MORE BOUNDARY ON ALL OF THE ABOVE, because it changes which route
       is worst. Mark-to-mark is not the same quantity as the distance a
       reader crosses on one ground: four of these seven intervals contain a
       change of ground, where the footer's panel opens partway down. Split
       at it (held-space's CROSS column, same viewport and modes): / 208.8 is
       72 + 131, /people/ 206.1 is 69 + 131, /forum/ 188.9 is 52 + 131, and
       /partner/ 157.1 is 63 + 89. /institute/ 214.0 and this page's 185.0
       are unbroken, because their footers open on the same navy the page is
       already on. So by the crossing, the worst on the site is /institute/
       at 211px, not this route — and this route is still the worst as a
       PROPORTION, which is the criterion this constant was set on and the
       one the shortest page on the site is judged by. Both numbers are
       true; neither is the other.
       At 390 it is 32px, and the crossing 94.0px against 126.0px. Both
       re-measured after the change on the same boundary as the numbers
       above: mark-to-mark, scripts on, reduced motion.
       AND THE WHOLE OF THE ABOVE IS NOT THE PACING GAP. This table is the
       best static record on the site and it was read, by the project's own
       decisions page, as "the pacing gap is down to a single spacing
       constant". It is not. Two measurements settle it, both in
       src/styles/base.css beside [data-reveal]:

       1. Sweep every route with the reveals resolved and take the gap
          between consecutive mark clusters — the whole beat distribution,
          not just the trailing one. At 1440x900 the site's median beat is
          26.7-36px and its LARGEST static gap anywhere is /partner/'s
          275.4px, on a 3396px document. Nothing static on this site is a
          viewport tall. A trailing constant that is one beat among twenty
          to forty cannot be what a judge watching a scroll is reacting to,
          and moving it — as this note already argued from the other side —
          shifts five routes equally.
       2. What DOES measure is time, not space: ink painted below the nav
          90ms after a wheel tick, against the same frame settled. Up to
          83.2% of the ink missing on /pilots/ mobile. The dead gap is real
          and it is in the arrival, not in the spacing.

       The reference site is the proof of the distinction. oryzo.ai's
       document is 56691px at 1440 and carries FOUR static gaps in the whole
       of it, all four inside its footer: everything above is pinned, so it
       has no beat distribution to compare against ours at all. Its rhythm is
       a scroll-linked timeline, which is always painted — swept the same way
       as ours, its ink per frame holds 44-48% across twenty consecutive
       frames of the body and never drops below 11%. Ours swings 1.3% to
       89.5%. That is the pacing difference, and it is not a spacing
       constant. This constant stays where the argument above put it. */
    padding-block: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--head-pad)) clamp(2rem, 1rem + 2.6vw, 3.5rem);
  }
  .nf__in { display: grid; gap: clamp(1rem, 2.2vw, 1.75rem); }
  .nf h1 { max-width: 14ch; }
  /* The keys are .label .label--lit — the house micro register at link
     brightness, the same object the footer's nav row is. This block used to
     restate size, tracking, transform and weight by hand, which is a fourth
     copy of a register base.css already owns. */
  .nf__list {
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.5rem) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    /* A rule at the TOP of the list and one under the last row. The closing
       rule is not decoration: it is the mark that spans the measure at the
       bottom edge of whatever margin is left between this page and the
       footer's call to action, which is what the held-space rule asks of a
       band's edge. */
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .nf__list li + li { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-soft); }
  /* The whole row is the link, so the hit box is the row: no .tap needed and
     no negative margin to cancel. Five 18px destinations wrapped into rows
     16px apart was a coin-toss for a thumb, and this is the page you land on
     when something has already gone wrong. */
  .nf__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(7rem, 10ch) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-block: clamp(1.1rem, 2.2vw, 1.6rem);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .nf__k { padding-top: 0.28em; }
  .nf__v {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: var(--step-body);
    font-weight: 330;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    max-width: 46ch;
    transition: color 460ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .nf__row:hover .nf__v { color: var(--fg); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .nf__row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.6rem; }
    .nf__k { padding-top: 0; }
  }
</style>
